# A rehearsal of `acme-dns`, against Let's Encrypt's staging directory.
#
# ── Why this mode exists ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Production Let's Encrypt allows five *duplicate* certificates per week for the
# same exact set of names, with no override and no appeal. Five is generous
# right up until it is not: a reinstall that discards the `caddy-data` volume
# asks for a fresh certificate every time, so an afternoon of "wipe it and try
# again" spends the week's quota and then locks the name out until it rolls off
# — a wait measured in days, on the address the whole deployment answers at.
#
# Staging has no meaningful limit. Use it to prove the whole chain works — DNS
# resolves here, port 80 is reachable, the challenge completes, the app answers
# through the proxy — and only then switch to `acme-dns` for a real one.
#
# ── The cost, and it is the entire point ──────────────────────────────────────
#
# Staging is signed by an untrusted root, so **every browser will warn**, and
# because the origin is not secure the app genuinely does not work: no Secure
# cookie, no service worker. Verify with `curl -k` and read the Caddy log; do
# not judge this mode from a browser.
#
# ── Switching to the real certificate ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   METACLAUDE_TLS_MODE=acme-dns   in .env, then `docker compose up -d`
#
# Caddy keys stored certificates by issuer URL, so changing the directory makes
# it request a fresh one from production rather than reusing the staging cert.
# Nothing needs clearing by hand.
#
# ── Why not a variable on acme-dns ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `tls <email>` uses Caddy's *default issuer pair*, which falls back to a second
# CA when the first refuses. Naming `issuer acme` to make the directory
# configurable would silently drop that fallback from the production path in
# exchange for a knob only ever used while rehearsing. A separate mode keeps
# `acme-dns` exactly as it is.
tls {
	issuer acme {
		dir https://acme-staging-v02.api.letsencrypt.org/directory
		email {$METACLAUDE_TLS_EMAIL}
	}
}
